{
	title: 'Expand or collapse blocks: /expand, /collapse, lists, live hint, Tab',
	spec: `/toggle, /expand and /collapse, and their dialog, on the terminal and the web alike. The user-facing term is "block"; its states are expanded and collapsed. The dialog title is "Expand or collapse blocks".

Syntax (shared by all three commands): a list of items separated by commas, spaces or both (t14,t16,t30-45 and #t14 16 30-45 are the same). An item is an optional '#', optional kind letters, then a number, a range N-M (inclusive) or '*'. Kinds: t tool, r reasoning (thinking), a assistant, u user, m message from another session, s system, q question. Letters combine: au10-40 means the user and assistant blocks in 10-40; t* means every tool block. A single number names one block of any kind; a letter before it is ignored (#t24, t24, 24 are the same). A range or '*' without letters never touches user or assistant blocks; naming a or u includes them. An empty target is the latest tool block.

/expand expands every matched block and /collapse collapses them; neither name may be confused with /close, which closes the tab. /toggle flips one named block; for anything more (a range, '*' or a list) it applies the majority rule to the whole match: expand all when more than half are collapsed, otherwise collapse all; a tie expands, since expanding hides nothing. Fold state stays per client, so each client applies /toggle's rule to its own state.

Dialog (Ctrl-O, runs /toggle): the field; below it a live hint that updates on every keystroke and says what Enter does ("Expands 12 tool blocks: t4-t88") or why the input is invalid; then a help area:
  24, #t24   one block          t*   all tool blocks
  10-24      a range            r*   all thinking (reasoning) blocks
  t14, r2-9  a list             Tab  complete a block id
  empty      latest tool block
  User and assistant blocks change only when you name them (a, u).
  Kinds: tool, reasoning, assistant, user, message, system, question
The kinds row separates words with commas, like the terminal's key help row; each word's first letter (the kind letter) is bright and bold. The placeholder is static ("24, 10-24 or t*").

Tab completes like /command completion: it fills the common prefix of the matching block ids for the item under the cursor; when several match it lists them in the help area, packed several per row, newest first, at least 10; more than fit end with "+N more". It never cycles.

Size: the terminal dialog is as tall as its content (field, hint, help or completions); the web dialog is as tall as its content too, not the full picker height.

Typed by the user (or Ctrl-O), the client runs the command itself, unrecorded. Run by the model through the command tool, the host resolves the target against history, tells the following clients to apply it, and returns the matched blocks, each with its model and effort where history records them ("Expanded 3 thinking blocks: r12 (opus-5-5, xhigh), r40 (opus-5-5, xhigh), r77 (opus-5-5, high)"), so the model can narrow a request such as "expand the xhigh thinking blocks" into a list. /toggle's result says "Toggled" with the same list, since the outcome depends on each client's state.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"The parser and dialog logic live in src/common (toggle.ts: parse, resolve, majority rule; blocks-dialog.ts: live hint, Tab completion, packed id rows) so both clients share them; the host's src/host/session-blocks.ts gives /go and /toggle the same block list. Tab reuses completion.common from the /command completer rather than a copy.",
		'Web folds.set must use updater setters: /expand t* sets several blocks in one batch, and reading the signal inside untrack saw the pre-batch state, so only the last block expanded.',
		"The web compact Picker needs height: fit-content; height: auto still filled the dialog's fixed picker height."
	],
	needs: ['ghs', 'r4d'],
	foldInto: ['ghs']
}
